The [Rijksmuseum](https://www.rijksmuseum.nl/en) holds around eight thousand objects across eighty rooms, which is more than anyone takes in on one visit, and the museum knows it. Their own advice is that you can see the highlights in an hour. Treat that as the floor rather than the ceiling: an afternoon is enough to do the Gallery of Honour properly and still have the energy to wander.

## Start at the top, then let it go

Most people head straight up to the Gallery of Honour on the second floor, and they are right to. Vermeer's Milkmaid, Frans Hals, Jan Steen, and at the far end of it Rembrandt's Night Watch, hung where the building was designed to put it. See that first, while you are still fresh.

After that, stop trying to be thorough. The museum is open daily from nine to five and a start time has to be booked, so the shape of the afternoon is set before you arrive: pick a floor, walk it slowly, and skip the rest without guilt. The delftware, the dolls' houses and the model ships reward an unhurried look far more than a dutiful one.

The gardens at the back are free and open to anyone, with or without a ticket. That is worth knowing on a warm day, and worth knowing when your feet have had enough.

## Where to stop for coffee

- **[RIJKS restaurant](https://rijksrestaurant.nl/)**, inside the museum on Museumstraat. Rather more than a coffee stop, but you can sit down without a ticket, and it is the easiest place to regroup mid-visit.
- **Het Café**, in the museum itself. Coffee and cake, no ceremony, and you do not have to leave the building or lose your place.
- **The Garden House**, out in the museum gardens: a quiet spot for a coffee among the sculpture, and the only one on this list where the room is the garden.
- **[Cobra Café](https://www.cobracafe.nl/)**, on Museumplein in front of the museum. A terrace facing the lawn, useful when you would rather look at the square than at another painting.
- **[Café Wildschut](https://www.cafewildschut.nl/)**, a few minutes south on Roelof Hartplein. A grand cafe in an Amsterdam School building, far enough from the museum crowd to feel like the afternoon has moved on.

## What to keep an eye out for

The permanent collection is the reason to come, but the Philips Wing runs temporary exhibitions on their own tickets, and those are worth checking before you book so you can take both in one start time. The free museum app carries several routes through the building, including a short one if you are running out of afternoon. Late in the day is the quietest the galleries get, and the last hour before five is the closest you will come to having the Night Watch to yourself.

The honest version of an afternoon in the Rijksmuseum: book a start time, walk the Gallery of Honour while you are fresh, give up on completeness, and finish in the garden.
